# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx mutate --list
vx mutate suite [mutant ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

A suite that passes says the code is right only if the suite would fail on wrong code.
`vx mutate` runs a suite on a copy of the tree with one edit made, an edit that removes a safeguard, and says whether the suite failed.

<suite> is the directory of a mutants file, or the name its `suite` line gives; `--list` prints each, with its directory and how many mutants it has.
Any <mutant> names pick those to run; with none, all run.
A suite is a SwiftPM test target, run alone: on Linux by `swift test --filter`, on macOS by building the target and running its Swift Testing bundle through the toolchain's `swiftpm-testing-helper` (see vx-build(1), `vx test --host`).
Run it from the repository's root.

The tree is copied, with the changes in the working tree, to `build/vx/mutate/<suite>/`, which later runs keep and bring up to date by copying only what differs, so the build in it is incremental.
The working tree is never edited.
A run that was killed leaves its edit in the copy only, and the next run copies it away.

The suite runs in the copy unchanged, a control, then once for each mutant: its edit made, the suite run, the edit reverted.
The control runs again after every fifth mutant, and last.
A control that fails ends the run with a message saying so, since on a busy machine a failure is the machine's and no mutant's catch.

Each mutant ends one of four ways:

: caught
  the suite failed.
: survived, explained
  it passed, and the mutant's `survives` says why that is right.
: survived
  it passed, with no reason given.
: did nothing
  it passed, and the lines of its output that start with the file's `measure` are those of the control's: the edit changed nothing the suite looks at.
  Only a suite with a `measure` can end so.

A mutant whose `find` isn't in its file exactly once, or whose edit doesn't build, is an error: it is neither caught nor survived.
`vx mutate` exits 1 if any mutant survived without a reason, did nothing or was an error, or a control failed.

It prints a line for each run, with its name, how it ended, the seconds, and the first line of the failure that caught it or the reason it survives.
Each run's whole output is in `build/vx/mutate.log`.

# FILES

: `<directory>/mutants`
  A suite's mutants file, beside the suite.
  Its lines start with a keyword, then a space and the rest of the line; blank lines and lines starting `#` are skipped.
  `suite NAME` names the test target, once, first.
  `measure PREFIX`, once, names the output lines that measure a run, such as `ring check:`; the lines that start with it must be the same in every control.
  Each `mutant NAME` (one word) starts an edit, with `in PATH` (from the repository's root), `find TEXT` (which must occur once in the file), `with TEXT` (its replacement, which may be empty), `why TEXT` (the safeguard it removes), and, only for an edit expected to survive, `survives TEXT`.
  In `find` and `with`, `\n`, `\t` and `\\` are a new line, a tab and a backslash.
  Errors in the file are reported with its name and line.
: `build/vx/mutate/<suite>/`
  The copy, with its own `.build`.
: `build/vx/mutate.log`
  The output of each run.

